  "issue_number": 15691,
  "issue_title": "Vapor: template abbreviation around `<form>` nests the form's next sibling inside it and drops a later `<form>`",
  "issue_body": "### Vue version\n\n3.6.0-rc.9 (the same strings on `minor` at bd6b9caa)\n\n### Link to minimal reproduction\n\nhttps://play.vuejs.org/#\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nOpen the playground in Chrome. `ShapesVdom.vue` and `ShapesVapor.vue` render the same template and print the `outerHTML` of its two `<section>` roots:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <section ref=\"a\">\n    <form><div>x</div></form>\n    <p>y</p>\n  </section>\n  <section ref=\"b\">\n    <div><form><input name=\"a\" /></form></div>\n    <div><form><input name=\"b\" /></form></div>\n  </section>\n  <pre>{{ html }}</pre>\n</template>\n```\n\nThe \"mount Crash.vue\" button mounts a Vapor component with the first shape and a binding in the `<p>`.\n\nThe compiler side alone:\n\n```js\nimport { compile } from '@vue/compiler-vapor'\n\nconst templates = (source) => [...compile(source).ast.template.keys()]\ntemplates('<section><form><div>x</div></form><p>y</p></section>')\n// [ '<section><form><div>x</form><p>y' ]\n```\n\n### What is expected?\n\nThe Vapor component renders the tree the VDOM one does:\n\n```html\n<section><form><div>x</div></form><p>y</p></section>\n<section><div><form><input name=\"a\"></form></div><div><form><input name=\"b\"></form></div></section>\n```\n\n### What is actually happening?\n\n| template | emitted string | DOM from `template()`, Chrome 153 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `<section><form><div>x</div></form><p>y</p></section>` | `<section><form><div>x</form><p>y` | `<section><form><div>x<p>y</p></div></form></section>` |\n| `<section><div><form><input name=\"a\"></form></div><div><form><input name=\"b\"></form></div></section>` | `<section><div><form><input name=a></div><div><form><input name=b>` | `<section><div><form><input name=\"a\"></form></div><div><input name=\"b\"></div></section>` |\n\nThe `<p>` lands inside the form's `<div>`, and the second `<form>` is dropped while its input stays. The same happens with `<div><b>x</b></div>` or `<div>x</div><Comp />` as the form's content. With a binding after the form, `<section><form><div>x</div></form><p>{{ msg }}</p></section>`, the render function takes `next(form)`, gets `null`, and mounting throws:\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'firstChild')\n```\n\nOnly client-created trees are affected. SSR hydration adopts the server DOM and parses no template string, so a page hydrates fine and breaks when it is rendered on the client, e.g. after a client-side navigation.\n\n### System Info\n\n```shell\nSystem:\n  OS: macOS 27.0\n  CPU: arm64\nBinaries:\n  Node: 24.19.0\n  pnpm: 11.22.0\nBrowsers:\n  Chrome: 153.0.8010.54\nnpmPackages:\n  vue: 3.6.0-rc.9\n  @vue/compiler-vapor: 3.6.0-rc.9\n```\n\n### Any additional comments?\n\n**Cause.** `template()` builds the DOM with `<template>.innerHTML`: a fragment parse whose context element is a `template`, with only the root `html` element on the stack of open elements. There the shipping parsers handle `form` through the form element pointer:\n\n- `</form>` removes the form element alone. An element inside it whose end tag was omitted stays open, and the form's next sibling is inserted into it.\n- A `<form>` start tag is ignored while the pointer is set. A form closed by its parent's end tag leaves the pointer set.\n\nThis is the mechanism of #14336, which #14337 (3.6.0-beta.4) fixed for a `<button>` as the form's last child by adding `ALWAYS_CLOSE_TAGS`. Any other element as the form's last child still hits it, and `form` itself is not in that list. #15657 (open) covers the same class for foreign content.\n\n**Standard and browsers.** whatwg/html#12911 (merged 2026-09-07, fixes whatwg/html#12257, tests web-platform-tests/wpt#62488) makes a fragment parse with a `template` context element \"parsing template contents\", where the pointer is ignored and `</form>` pops elements until a form is popped; under it these strings parse as their templates nest. Chromium implements that behind the experimental `CorrectTemplateFormParsing` runtime flag (crbug.com/352896478): Chrome 153 run with `--enable-blink-features=CorrectTemplateFormParsing` parses every string above as its template nests and mounts the binding case. Stable Chrome applies the old rule, and so does the jsdom `DOMParser` document parse that `abbreviation.spec.ts` checks against, under either version of the standard, since no `template` is on the stack there. The fix below is correct under both rules.\n\n**Proposed fix.** Put `form` in `ALWAYS_CLOSE_TAGS`, so a form off the rightmost path keeps its end tag and resets the pointer, and keep the end tag of every element inside a form whose end tag is emitted:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/packages/compiler-vapor/__tests__/abbreviation.spec.ts b/packages/compiler-vapor/__tests__/abbreviation.spec.ts\n--- a/packages/compiler-vapor/__tests__/abbreviation.spec.ts\n+++ b/packages/compiler-vapor/__tests__/abbreviation.spec.ts\n@@ -288,3 +288,31 @@ test('inline/block ancestor relationships', () => {\n     '<div><span><div><span><div>text</div></span></div></span><p>after</p></div>',\n   )\n })\n+\n+test('form end tag', () => {\n+  // </form> removes only the form: a descendant left open would swallow the\n+  // form's next sibling\n+  checkAbbr(\n+    '<div><form><div>x</div></form><p>y</p></div>',\n+    '<div><form><div>x</div></form><p>y',\n+    '<div><form><div>x</div></form><p>y</p></div>',\n+  )\n+  checkAbbr(\n+    '<div><form><div><b>x</b></div></form><p>y</p></div>',\n+    '<div><form><div><b>x</b></div></form><p>y',\n+    '<div><form><div><b>x</b></div></form><p>y</p></div>',\n+  )\n+  // a form closed by its parent's end tag keeps the form element pointer set,\n+  // so the next <form> start tag is ignored\n+  checkAbbr(\n+    '<div><div><form><input></form></div><div><form><input></form></div></div>',\n+    '<div><div><form><input></form></div><div><form><input>',\n+    '<div><div><form><input></form></div><div><form><input></form></div></div>',\n+  )\n+  // rightmost path can still omit\n+  checkAbbr(\n+    '<div><p>y</p><form><div>x</div></form></div>',\n+    '<div><p>y</p><form><div>x',\n+    '<div><p>y</p><form><div>x</div></form></div>',\n+  )\n+})\ndiff --git a/packages/compiler-vapor/src/transforms/transformElement.ts b/packages/compiler-vapor/src/transforms/transformElement.ts\n--- a/packages/compiler-vapor/src/transforms/transformElement.ts\n+++ b/packages/compiler-vapor/src/transforms/transformElement.ts\n@@ -259,9 +259,13 @@ function canOmitEndTag(\n     return true\n   }\n \n+  // `</form>` removes only the form element itself (it goes through the form\n+  // element pointer), so elements inside a form whose end tag is emitted must\n+  // close themselves.\n   if (\n     (context.templateCloseTags &&\n       (context.templateCloseTags.has(node.tag) ||\n+        context.templateCloseTags.has('form') ||\n         isAlwaysCloseTag(node.tag) ||\n         isFormattingTag(node.tag))) ||\n     (context.templateCloseBlocks && isBlockTag(node.tag))\ndiff --git a/packages/shared/src/domTagConfig.ts b/packages/shared/src/domTagConfig.ts\n--- a/packages/shared/src/domTagConfig.ts\n+++ b/packages/shared/src/domTagConfig.ts\n@@ -48,7 +48,7 @@ const FORMATTING_TAGS = 'a,b,big,code,em,font,i,nobr,s,small,strike,strong,tt,u'\n // - Scope boundary elements\n const ALWAYS_CLOSE_TAGS =\n   'title,style,script,noscript,template,' + // raw text / special parsing\n-  'object,table,button,textarea,select,iframe,fieldset' // scope boundary / form elements\n+  'object,table,button,textarea,select,iframe,fieldset,form' // scope boundary / form elements\n \n // Inline elements\n const INLINE_TAGS =\n```\n\nOn `minor` at bd6b9caa the new test fails without the patch and without either half of it, and passes with it; the `compiler-vapor`, `shared`, `runtime-vapor`, `compiler-sfc` and `compiler-ssr` unit tests pass (135 files). The patched build emits `<section><form><div>x</div></form><p>y` and `<section><div><form><input name=a></form></div><div><form><input name=b>`, which stock Chrome 153 parses as the templates nest. The cost is extra end tags inside forms off the rightmost path.\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-09-28T03:48:59Z",
